            "question_id": 13717,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 278,
            "sort": 2,
            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"N0Dfd70vKo3C16x22MllfNEwg8e\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-V3BBddJOMoIvXLxSbwElWOqTgte\">Overall, there was a shift towards smaller households in the UK over this 20-year period, with an increase in the proportions of 1- and 2-person households, while the other groups showed a downward trend. Moreover, in both years, the most common living arrangement comprised of 2 individuals.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13717,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 279,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"N0Dfd70vKo3C16x22MllfNEwg8e\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-XH8BdSde7ongl9xyLb0lBP5Mgxc\">Starting with smaller households, in 1981, single-person households accounted for 17%, only half of those with 2 residents, at 31%. This highest proportion was followed by 3-person and 4-person households, with figures of 20% and 18% respectively, while the percentages for those with 5 and 6 occupants were significantly lower, standing at 8% and 6% in that order.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13717,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 280,
            "sort": 4,
            "name": "Body 2",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"N0Dfd70vKo3C16x22MllfNEwg8e\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-BmGsdeR85o4rmUxH4rxlQ0QOgad\">After 20 years, the figure for the household size of 2 members increased slightly by 3%, much lower than the growth rate of single-person households, surging by 9% and surpassing 3- and 4-person households. These latter two categories saw decreases of 3% each, to 17% and 15% respectively. Larger households, specifically those with 5 and 6 individuals, also experienced a decline, falling to 6% and 2% respectively by the end of the period.</div>\n</div>",
